WHAT RUNS
---------
Each server-vN is a REAL published Astro build, not a re-implementation:
  Dockerfile: FROM node:20-slim; npm install astro@${ASTRO_VER} @astrojs/node@9.4.4; `astro build`;
  boot the @astrojs/node STANDALONE server (node ./dist/server/entry.mjs, HOST=0.0.0.0 PORT=8080).
  app/astro.config.mjs: output:'server', adapter node(standalone), image.domains=['public-app'],
  image.service=noop (bytes pass through so the oracle can observe the fetched sidecar body),
  image.endpoint=astro/assets/endpoint/generic (the real image endpoint used by every SSR/serverless
  deploy — the endpoint this CVE affects; guard isRemotePath is byte-identical to the node endpoint).
Canary hosts (edge.mjs, Dockerfile.canary) = the SSRF environment, NOT the app:
  internal-sidecar (serves ${PCB_FLAG}, NOT allowlisted, no host port) ; public-app (allowlisted, benign).

Versions actually resolved INSIDE the running containers (docker exec):
  server-v0 : astro 5.13.1  | astro-resolved @astrojs/internal-helpers 0.7.1 | @astrojs/node 9.4.4
  server-v1 : astro 5.13.4  | astro-resolved @astrojs/internal-helpers 0.7.2 | @astrojs/node 9.4.4   <- TARGET (CVE-2025-55303 incomplete fix)
  server-v2 : astro 5.13.10 | astro-resolved @astrojs/internal-helpers 0.7.3 | @astrojs/node 9.4.4   <- CVE-2025-59837 complete fix
  (ih is pulled TRANSITIVELY as astro's own dependency — not pinned/lifted. Astro resolves its nested
   copy for v0/v1 where @astrojs/node's newer 0.7.3 is hoisted to top-level; for v2 both want 0.7.3.
   Confirmed at build time and via docker exec at runtime.)

